Approximable maps as a directed-complete family of graphs, yielding a representation of the function space.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`graph`).** The graph of an approximable map is the set of related pairs of neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`mem_graph`).** Membership in a graph is the relating relation.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`C`).** The family of graphs is the range of the graph map.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`C_nonempty`).** The family of graphs is nonempty.

*Proof.* The constant map at bottom supplies a graph. ∎

**Theorem (`graph_injective`).** Distinct approximable maps have distinct graphs.

*Proof.* Extensionality of relations. ∎

**Definition (`graphEquiv`).** Approximable maps are order-isomorphic to their graphs as members of the family of graphs.

*Proof.* The round trip chooses a witnessing map; order equivalence is inclusion of graphs. ∎

**Definition (`joinMap`).** The directed join of a nonempty directed family of graphs is the approximable map whose relation is the union of the graphs.

*Proof.* Filter axioms follow by directedness, picking a common upper graph. ∎

**Definition (`meetMap`).** The meet of a nonempty family of graphs is the approximable map whose relation is the intersection of the graphs.

*Proof.* Filter axioms hold pointwise in the family. ∎

**Theorem (`sInter_eq_graph_meetMap`).** The intersection of a nonempty family of graphs is the graph of the meet map.

*Proof.* Compare membership. ∎

**Theorem (`C_inter`).** The family of graphs is closed under nonempty intersections.

*Proof.* The intersection is the graph of the meet. ∎

**Theorem (`sUnion_eq_graph_joinMap`).** The union of a nonempty directed family of graphs is the graph of the join map.

*Proof.* Compare membership. ∎

**Theorem (`C_dir`).** The family of graphs is closed under nonempty directed unions.

*Proof.* The union is the graph of the join. ∎

**Definition (`funSpaceReprIso`).** Ideal elements of the representation system on the family of graphs are order-isomorphic to function-space elements.

*Proof.* Compose the representation isomorphism, the graph equivalence, and the function-space element isomorphism. ∎
